{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AxpbxiMsS3Jwxq15xZG8DE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xf1rDCKV49AEkHGSXWQFSo","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Full Roundtable\n\n**Paper:** \"Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 31% Temporal Instability in MMLU: A Multi-Method Investigation\"\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n**Review mode:** full (2 Reviewers → 4-step roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### Reviewer 1 — Methods & Experiments\n\n**Summary.** The paper applies MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items), finding 31.3% ETS Category C items between 2023–2024 cohorts vs 0.18% random baseline. It proposes a semi-synthetic injection framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) and conducts ~13 complementary analyses. The scale is impressive and the ground truth meta-critique (enrichment≈0.96 against Li et al. labels) is a genuine conceptual contribution, but several foundational concerns limit the paper's impact.\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Largest DIF study on LLM benchmarks to date (5,227×12,508). Random-split control (0.18%) provides a clean null.\n- The global-vs-differential contamination distinction is well-argued and useful for the contamination detection community.\n- Cross-subject matching protocol is methodologically sound — the algebraic invariance guarantee (ΔFPR=0.000) is a clean result.\n- Appropriate hedging on exploratory analyses (GSM1K N=33 labeled as suggestive). Rank-order stability (ρ=0.997) is correctly interpreted as diagnostic, not destructive.\n- Cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (C%=18.9%) strengthens generalizability.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **(Major) Ability confounding unresolved.** The drop from 31.3% (temporal) to 2.9% (ability-quintile) is the most important number in the paper but receives insufficient attention. Nearest-neighbor matching reduces C% by only 2.4 pp (to 28.9%), but the ~26 pp gap between 28.9% and 2.9% remains unexplained. The paper attributes it to \"genuine item-level DIF\" but cannot distinguish this from unmeasured confounds correlated with temporal cohort membership (training methodology shifts, data composition changes, alignment changes). This interpretive gap is acknowledged in the Limitations but should be more prominently reflected in the main claims.\n- **(Major) 98% IRT model misfit undermines theoretical grounding.** MH-DIF conditions on total score as a proxy for unidimensional θ. With 98% of models showing |Lz|>2 and the bifactor model failing to converge (48h, RMSEA>0.10, CFI<0.80), the unidimensional assumption is fundamentally violated. The paper notes r(θ, total_score)=0.9986, but this shows only that θ and total score are near-identical, not that either is a valid ability proxy for a 57-subject benchmark. DIF flags may reflect multidimensionality artifacts rather than genuine item-level instability.\n- **(Major) Breadth at the cost of depth.** ~13 distinct analyses in 8 pages, many reported in 1–2 sentences. The paper reads as an experimental catalog rather than a focused investigation. Key analyses (ability matching, semi-synthetic framework, GSM1K correlation) deserve more space; peripheral analyses (architecture stratification as a tautology given 99.98% decoder-only, Bayesian cumulative DIF as pure simulation, item generation below thresholds) should be cut or relegated to appendix.\n- **(Minor) Semi-synthetic injection is simplistic.** Flipping incorrect→correct with probability p caricatures real contamination (partial memorization, template matching, reasoning shortcuts). The 31.4% baseline FPR on \"clean\" items raises questions about what DIF measures absent intentional contamination.\n- **(Minor) GSM1K correlation (N=33) carries limited inferential weight.** Wide CI (upper |r|=0.155 for Pearson), max Cook's D on Deepseek-math-7b-rl. Partial r=−0.385 is borderline. This should not feature as prominently as it does.\n- **(Minor) Within-family results are hard to interpret.** Llama-2 vs Llama-3 at C%=61.3% (d=−0.99) — after ability matching drops to 28.3%, consistent with temporal matched rate. Adds little beyond temporal analysis while potentially confusing readers.\n\n**Questions for authors:**\n1. What accounts for the ~26 pp gap between ability-matched C% (28.9%) and ability-quintile C% (2.9%)? Can you formally decompose the temporal DIF signal into ability-driven, methodology-driven, and residual components?\n2. Given 98% IRT misfit and failed bifactor convergence, how confident are you that MH-DIF produces valid item-level flags rather than multidimensionality artifacts?\n3. Why not include finer-grained dose-response values (p=0.1, 0.2) to characterize the minimum detectable effect size?\n4. If ρ=0.997 after removing all DIF items, what is the actionable recommendation for benchmark maintainers?\n5. If DIF, ConStat, and DVD disagree (Jaccard≤0.11, ensemble AUROC=0.59), what confidence should we have in any individual method?\n\n**Scores:**\n- Soundness: 3.0\n- Excitement: 3.0\n- Reproducibility: 4.0\n- Overall: 3.0 (Findings)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer 2 — Novelty & Related Work\n\n**Summary.** The paper applies the standard MH-DIF procedure (Holland & Thayer, 1988) to MMLU via METABENCH and finds 31% temporal DIF, then conducts ~13 complementary analyses. The ground truth meta-critique (enrichment≈0.96) and semi-synthetic cross-subject matching are useful but modest contributions. The paper's own controls (ability-matching reduces to 2.9%; ρ=0.997 rank stability) substantially weaken the headline finding. The novelty bar for EMNLP is not met.\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Empirical thoroughness within the primary analysis: multiple controls (random split, ability-quintile, within-family, nearest-neighbor matching) provide meaningful context.\n- Ground truth meta-critique is the strongest conceptual contribution — the global-vs-differential contamination distinction could influence future validation practices.\n- Semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is methodologically clean and reusable.\n- Honest reporting of limitations and negative results (bifactor failure, item generation below threshold, ensemble near chance).\n- METABENCH is public; MH-DIF is standard. Reproduction is straightforward.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- **(Major) Core application is textbook method transfer.** MH-DIF is a 1988 procedure applied without modification. The paper states \"we do not modify the MH-DIF procedure itself.\" Applying an off-the-shelf method to a new dataset, finding nonzero results, and then showing the results don't affect rankings is below the EMNLP novelty bar. The empirical contribution must stand on surprise value, but the paper's own controls dissolve the surprise.\n- **(Major) Related work is thin and missing critical references.** Only ~10 references substantively discussed in Section 2. Key omissions: (1) IRT in NLP beyond Lalor et al. — Rodriguez et al. 2021 (\"Evaluation Examples Are Not Equally Informative\"), Byrd & Srivastava 2022 on IRT for NLU benchmarks; (2) test equating literature — the paper's central concern (temporal comparability) is precisely what test equating addresses, yet common-item equating, IRT-based equating, equipercentile equating are never discussed; (3) broader contamination detection — Yang et al. 2023, Oren et al. 2024, Deng et al. 2024; (4) measurement invariance in ML fairness literature.\n- **(Major) Framing mismatch between title and content.** The title says \"31% Temporal Instability\" but the paper's own ability-quintile control yields 2.9%, and nearest-neighbor matching yields 28.9%. Leading with the uncontrolled 31.3% in the title and abstract while burying the nuance is misleading. A more accurate framing would foreground the ability-matched figure.\n- **(Major) Too many experiments at insufficient depth.** ~13 analyses in 8 pages creates a catalog, not a contribution. Key analyses (ability matching, semi-synthetic framework, GSM1K) are compressed to single sentences. Peripheral analyses (architecture stratification is tautological given 99.98% decoder-only; Bayesian cumulative DIF is simulation-only; DVD yields 7% detection and minimal overlap; item generation fails below thresholds) waste space. The paper would improve by dropping 6–8 experiments and developing the core 3–4 properly.\n- **(Major) Novelty claims overstated.** The introduction's \"three layers of genuine novelty\" is generous: (a) finding 31% from an existing method on new data is an empirical observation, not a methodological contribution; (b) web-overlap labels as wrong ground truth is a negative result (enrichment≈1.0); (c) semi-synthetic framework is straightforward inject-and-measure.\n- **(Minor) GSM1K correlation underpowered and over-featured.** N=33, r=−0.478, CI=[−0.71, −0.16]. Appropriately labeled \"suggestive\" in-text but features in the abstract and conclusion as a primary evidence pillar.\n- **(Minor) \"Instability that doesn't matter\" framing neutralizes the contribution.** If ρ=0.997 means rankings are unaffected, why should practitioners care? The paper needs to choose: either instability is consequential (foreground family-specific rank shifts) or inconsequential (then the paper reports a non-problem).\n- **(Minor) Missing engagement with \"improvement vs instability.\"** Within-family Llama-2 vs Llama-3 at C%=61.3% strongly suggests DIF detects capability improvement, not benchmark pathology. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but does not grapple with the implication.\n\n**Questions for authors:**\n1. Can you provide a formal decomposition of 31.3% into ability-driven, training-methodology-driven, and residual components? The pseudo-R²=0.0007 decomposition using domain-level accuracy is too coarse.\n2. What is the actionable recommendation? \"Psychometric monitoring\" is vague.\n3. How does DIF relate to Growing Pains' FPC-based anchor item identification? What is the overlap?\n4. Why not use logistic regression DIF as the primary method, given it detects non-uniform DIF (24.8%) and is more informative?\n5. 98% IRT misfit and r(θ, total_score)=0.9986 — does this not suggest IRT adds nothing beyond raw total score, undermining the psychometric framing?\n\n**Scores:**\n- Soundness: 3.0\n- Excitement: 2.0\n- Reproducibility: 4.0\n- Overall: 2.5 (Borderline Findings)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 → R2 key points shared:**\n- The ability confounding gap (28.9% matched vs 2.9% ability-quintile) is the paper's Achilles heel — ~26 pp unexplained\n- 98% IRT misfit makes all MH-DIF flags potentially suspect as multidimensionality artifacts\n- Semi-synthetic baseline FPR of 31.4% on \"clean\" items is concerning — it mirrors the 31% temporal C%, suggesting the method has a ~31% base rate regardless\n- Cross-subject matching is the cleanest methodological contribution\n\n**R2 → R1 key points shared:**\n- The novelty bar is not met: textbook method transfer without modification\n- Related work has serious gaps (test equating, IRT-in-NLP, broader contamination literature)\n- The framing is misleading: title advertises the uncontrolled 31% while controls reduce it dramatically\n- The paper tries to be three things (methods paper, empirical paper, meta-analysis) and doesn't succeed at any\n- \"Instability that doesn't matter\" framing is self-defeating\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Areas of agreement (strong consensus):**\n1. **Ability confounding is the central unresolved issue.** Both reviewers flag the 31.3%→2.9% drop as devastating and inadequately addressed. The ~26 pp gap between nearest-neighbor matched (28.9%) and ability-quintile (2.9%) remains unexplained.\n2. **IRT misfit undermines the framework.** 98% |Lz|>2, failed bifactor — the psychometric tool doesn't fit the data. Both agree this is more than a limitation.\n3. **Breadth over depth.** ~13 experiments in 8 pages produces a catalog, not a contribution. Both recommend cutting peripheral analyses.\n4. **Ground truth meta-critique is the strongest contribution.** The global-vs-differential distinction and enrichment≈0.96 are useful for the community.\n5. **Semi-synthetic cross-subject matching is clean.** ΔFPR=0.000 is a genuine methodological result.\n6. **GSM1K is underpowered.** N=33 should not feature as prominently as it does.\n7. **Below EMNLP main conference bar.** Both agree.\n\n**Areas of disagreement:**\n- **Excitement:** R1 gives 3.0 (the scale and multi-method approach have value); R2 gives 2.0 (textbook method transfer, self-undermining findings). After discussion, R2's argument is more compelling — the paper's own results dissolve its headline. **Converged: 2.5.**\n- **Overall:** R1 at 3.0 (Findings); R2 at 2.5 (Borderline Findings). The novelty concerns raised by R2 are substantive. The related work gaps are real and would need addressing. However, the ground truth meta-critique and semi-synthetic framework have value. **Converged: 2.5 (Borderline Findings).**\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Final Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Soundness | 3.0 |\n| Excitement | 2.5 |\n| Reproducibility | 4.0 |\n| **Overall** | **2.5 (Borderline Findings)** |\n\n### Meta-Review\n\nThis paper applies the standard MH-DIF procedure to the METABENCH MMLU response matrix at large scale (5,227 models × 12,508 items) and finds 31.3% of items show large temporal DIF. Both reviewers acknowledge the scale and the honest reporting of results, including negative ones. Two contributions have genuine value: (1) the meta-critique that web-overlap contamination labels (enrichment≈0.96) are inappropriate ground truth for differential item-level detectors, and (2) the semi-synthetic cross-subject matching framework (ΔFPR=0.000).\n\nHowever, both reviewers identify serious concerns that prevent recommendation for the main conference:\n\n**1. Ability confounding is unresolved and undermines the headline.** The 31.3% temporal C% drops to 2.9% under ability-quintile matching, and nearest-neighbor matching yields 28.9%. The paper cannot explain the ~26 pp gap between these two controlled estimates, and the 31% figure is presented as the headline despite being the least controlled. The framing — leading with 31% in the title and abstract while burying the controls — is misleading.\n\n**2. The psychometric framework doesn't fit the data.** 98% of models show IRT misfit (|Lz|>2), the bifactor model failed to converge, and the architecture stratification confirms the problem is multidimensionality (97.8% misfit in decoder-only subset). MH-DIF conditions on a unidimensional total score that is fundamentally misspecified for a 57-subject benchmark. All DIF flags may reflect multidimensionality artifacts rather than genuine item-level instability.\n\n**3. The paper's own results neutralize its contribution.** Ranking stability (ρ=0.997) means the instability doesn't matter for aggregate validity. The three detection methods disagree (Jaccard≤0.11, ensemble AUROC=0.59). The ensemble fails. The item generation falls below practical thresholds. The Bayesian cumulative DIF is simulation-only. The paper reports many negative results honestly, but collectively they suggest the research program has not produced an actionable contribution.\n\n**4. Novelty is insufficient for EMNLP.** Applying a 1988 textbook method without modification to a new dataset does not constitute a methodological contribution. The related work is thin, missing critical engagement with test equating, IRT-in-NLP, and the broader contamination detection literature. The ~13 experiments in 8 pages create a catalog rather than a coherent contribution — no individual analysis receives adequate depth.\n\n**5. The \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing is self-defeating.** If the paper's own graceful degradation analysis shows ρ>0.96 at 50% replacement, the practical significance of 31% instability is unclear. The paper oscillates between presenting the finding as important (title, abstract) and dismissing it as inconsequential (Discussion), failing to commit to a clear position.\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**2.5 — Borderline Findings.** The ground truth meta-critique and semi-synthetic framework are useful contributions that could anchor a more focused paper. For revision, the authors should: (1) foreground the ability-matched figures (28.9% or 2.9%) rather than the uncontrolled 31%; (2) formally decompose the DIF signal into ability-driven and residual components; (3) engage with the test equating literature and IRT-in-NLP work; (4) cut peripheral experiments (architecture stratification, DVD, Bayesian cumulative DIF, item generation) and develop the core 3–4 analyses properly; (5) address the IRT misfit concern more substantively than a limitations footnote. A focused paper on the meta-critique + semi-synthetic framework + honest primary DIF finding with proper controls would be a stronger Findings submission.\n\n### Issues List\n\n| # | Severity | Issue | Section |\n|---|----------|-------|---------|\n| I1 | Critical | Ability confounding: 31.3%→2.9% under ability-quintile matching; ~26 pp gap between matched estimates unexplained; title/abstract framing misleading | §4.1, Title, Abstract |\n| I2 | Critical | IRT misfit: 98% |Lz|>2, failed bifactor, multidimensionality threatens validity of all MH-DIF flags | §4.1, §4.7 |\n| I3 | Major | Breadth over depth: ~13 experiments in 8 pages; no analysis receives adequate treatment | §4 (all subsections) |\n| I4 | Major | Novelty below EMNLP bar: textbook method applied without modification | §3.1 |\n| I5 | Major | Related work thin: missing test equating, IRT-in-NLP (Rodriguez et al., Byrd & Srivastava), broader contamination literature | §2 |\n| I6 | Major | Self-defeating framing: ρ=0.997 means instability is inconsequential for rankings; practical actionability unclear | §4.10, §5.1 |\n| I7 | Moderate | GSM1K validation underpowered: N=33, wide CIs, influential outlier (Deepseek-math-7b-rl), over-featured in abstract | §4.6 |\n| I8 | Moderate | Semi-synthetic baseline FPR=31.4% on \"clean\" items mirrors the temporal C%, suggesting ~31% base rate | §4.13 |\n| I9 | Moderate | Ensemble failure (AUROC=0.59) and method disagreement (Jaccard≤0.11) raise questions about what any individual method captures | §4.5 |\n| I10 | Minor | RL robustness analysis underpowered (N=13); TOST p=0.09 fails conventional threshold | §4.2 |\n| I11 | Minor | Within-family results (Llama-2v3 at 61.3%) support \"DIF detects capability improvement\" reading | §4.1 |\n| I12 | Minor | Item-Focused Trees: moderate MH concordance (κ=0.61) + secondary splits on model_size/training_cutoff suggest DIF partially detects capability scaling | §4.11 |","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260521T085919","overall_score":"2.5 — Borderline Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5163,"cache_read_input_tokens":60757,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5163},"output_tokens":54,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"beaf218b-5fe0-4583-84e6-cda110d75429","uuid":"3223facb-d94d-49f8-87cf-13d8330f3be7"}
